#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097603} :activity/id #uuid "56d16c42-7bff-5947-a997-ebd77e5b8a2e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048424 :topic/id #uuid "9decebad-ba84-5d1a-9299-fa672cd17201" :topic/math-academy-id 1056} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097862 :tutorial/content "The **linear correlation coefficient,** denoted $\\rho$ (the Greek letter \"rho\"), is a number that gives the strength of the *linear* dependence between two sets of paired observations $(x,y)$.\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient satisfies the following constraint:\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe interpret the endpoints of this interval as follows:\n\n- $\\rho = 1$ means that there is a perfect *positive* correlation between the data points.\n- $\\rho = -1$ means that there is a perfect *negative* correlation between the data points.\n\nMoreover, $\\rho = 0$ means there is no *linear* correlation between the data points.\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is **strong,** and the data points closely follow a straight line.![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)\n\n- If $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is **weak.** The data points follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)\n\n- If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is **negligible.** There is no (linear) correlation.![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "372f5b4c-9826-52f6-a70c-5e3a035de64e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3699 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8f8585ea-c69b-57cc-9474-2e65fa5ad893" :step/math-academy-id 20623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096726 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096518 :question/id #uuid "aa7ce13a-2f5f-55f5-bac0-ced1a321d5b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5711"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2f40eb3-6087-5c38-b7c6-e160ff925bc6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097075}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Linear Correlation Coefficients in Context"} :step/id #uuid "389e6644-1f27-50f3-821c-27b03f4bc7d5" :step/math-academy-id 20625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096727 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096522 :question/id #uuid "5002af3e-d0f0-59eb-a52d-bfd7d101115e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5725"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76569338-1e2a-5b12-96ef-685d272343cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049976}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097073}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Correlation Coefficient Using Covariance and Standard Deviations"} :step/id #uuid "0575b9a0-d372-5de1-9411-b558a65a2b50" :step/math-academy-id 20709 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097695}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096593 :question/id #uuid "8c2381d6-a9af-5ce3-aa20-d0e19a7a7229" :question/math-academy-id "e-9710"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd2caf12-80c2-553a-9737-64151565cf86" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050406}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097077}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Linear Correlation Coefficient Using the Expanded Formula"} :step/id #uuid "d5b72b77-a57c-507c-b755-bdf8208dc2e8" :step/math-academy-id 36411 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097604}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097891 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nof the same size.\n\nLet's now introduce the following notation:\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \\qquad S_{yy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i-\\overline{y})^2, \\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y})\n$$\n\nNotice that $S_{xx}, S_{yy}$, and $S_{xy}$ are the sums of square deviations found in the formulas for the variance of $x$, the variance of $y$, and covariance of $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nUsing the previous definition, we can show that the correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ab8695a-49cb-5bc1-8934-ffd2ff28261e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5966 :tutorial/title "The Linear Correlation Coefficient in Sigma Notation"} :step/id #uuid "2e0466e2-c1f8-573f-bdcb-205404d8cec2" :step/math-academy-id 36415 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097902 :tutorial/content "To calculate $\\rho$, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance between $x$ and $y$, and\n- $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nFor example, consider data sets $x$ and $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)=-9, \\qquad \\sigma_x=6, \\qquad \\sigma_y=2\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= ((-9))/((6) \\cdot (2)) \\\\\n&= ((-9))/(12) \\\\\n&=-0.75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho$ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, there is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y$." :tutorial/id #uuid "6f296964-c1ff-5379-861d-1a9809ca7cc6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6586 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Correlation Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "c58771bb-e2f1-5f3c-9354-320e7568cc2d" :step/math-academy-id 39739 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097903 :tutorial/content "Let's derive the following result:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of the correlation coefficient $\\rho$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac1n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}) = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{n}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ_{x} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx}}{n}} \\\\\nσ_{y} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(y_{i} - y^(―))^{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{yy}}{n}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our definition for $\\rho$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx}}{n}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{yy}}{n}}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}{n^{2}}}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}{n})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "219dd7c3-97f9-5504-ad97-d8710a6cf5bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6587 :tutorial/title "Proving the Formula for Rho in Terms of Squared Deviations"} :step/id #uuid "be8ab20d-d46f-516f-aba3-1048baeb14b1" :step/math-academy-id 39740}] :activity/title "The Linear Correlation Coefficient" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096858}]}
